[[Image:Ground_Slam_skill_screenshot.jpg|thumb|{{il|Ground Slam|Ground Slam's}} "wave" area of effect]]

'''Area of effect''' (often referred to as '''AoE''') refers to mechanics (such as skills, auras, and monster abilities) that apply to an area of the ground (rather than targeting a particular character, monster, etc.).

Many AoE mechanics deal damage, but AoE mechanics can have other effects. For example, [[aura]]s produce positive effects on allies within the area, while [[curse]]s like {{il|Temporal Chains}} produce negative effects on enemies.

While AoE mechanics can affect multiple characters or monsters within the area, not all mechanics that can affect multiple characters or monsters are AoE mechanics. For example, {{il|Ice Spear}} targets a single monster rather than an area, so it is not an AoE skill, even though it creates [[Pierce|piercing]] [[projectile]]s that can hit other nearby monsters as well.

__TOC__

==Usage==
The term "area of effect" or "AoE" is used in several different ways:
* It can describe the mechanic itself. ("{{il|Molten Strike}} is an AoE skill.")
* It can describe the area affected by the mechanic. ("{{il|Frostblink}} creates a circular AoE at both ends of the teleport.")
* It can describe what the mechanic does to the characters or monsters within the area. ("[[Gruthkul, Mother of Despair|Gruthkul's]] slam attack does AoE physical damage.")

==Examples==
The simplest examples of AoE mechanics are skills like {{il|Shock Nova}}, {{il|Ice Nova}} and {{il|Sweep}}, which create circular areas of effect. Other AoE skills like {{il|Cleave}} and {{il|Ground Slam}} create cone-shaped areas of effect, while others like {{il|Flame Surge}} or {{il|Glacial Cascade}} have more complicated areas of effect.

==How modifiers to AoE sizes work== 
[[Support gem]]s and other mechanics can increase or decrease the size of an area of effect, almost always by a percentage, e.g. {{c|mod|30% increased Area of Effect}}. These percentages refer to the ''total amount of ground'' covered by the area of effect, not to the area of effect's radius, diameter, or other dimensions. For example, a {{c|mod|100% increased Area of Effect}} modifier applied to {{il|Shock Nova}}'s circular area of effect will double the area of ground covered by the circle. The mathematics of that mean that the circle's radius and diameter will increase by around 40%.

If multiple percentage-based modifiers apply to the same area of effect, they add together. For example, if {{c|mod|40% increased Area of Effect}}, {{c|mod|20% increased Area of Effect}}, and {{c|mod|30% decreased Area of Effect}} modifiers all apply to the same area of effect, the area of effect's size will increase by 30% (40+20-30).

A few mechanics apply a specific increase (usually expressed as an integer) to a dimension of the area of effect. For example, the skill gem level bonus for {{il|Caustic Arrow}} specifically grants a {{c|mod|+(0-6) to radius}} increase to the Caustic Arrow area of effect. These specific types of modifiers always apply before percentage-based modifiers are applied to the total area of effect. {{confirm}} This means that dimension-specific modifiers tend to have a greater impact on the area of effect's final size.

==Calculations==
Distances are displayed in metres, however all calculations are done using internal units equal to 0.1m. Any calculation that would produce a result that is not a multiple of 0.1m will be rounded down.

We can calculate the increased area of effect ({{Math|formula = AoE\%}}) required to increase a certain base radius {{Math|formula = r_b}} to a desired increased radius {{Math|formula = r_i}} using the formula for area of a circle:

{{Math|formula = \pi r_i^2 = \frac{100 + AoE\%}{100} \times \pi r_b^2 }}

Cancelling out {{Math|formula = \pi}} and dividing both sides by {{Math|formula = r_b^2}} we get

{{Math|formula = \frac{r_i^2}{r_b^2} = (\frac{r_i}{r_b})^2 = \frac{100 + AoE\%}{100} }}

Taking the square root of both sides shows that the proportion of the increased radius to the base radius is equal to the square root of the multiplier to area.

{{Math|formula = \frac{r_i}{r_b} = \sqrt{ \frac{100 + AoE\%}{100} } }}

This suggests that an area's radius can be increased from '''28''' to '''30''' with 15% increased area of effect:

{{Math|formula = (\frac{30}{28})^2 \approx 1.148 < \frac{100 + 15}{100} }}

'''However''', the square root of increased AoE is truncated to two decimal places before multiplying the radius, thus 15% increased AoE only results in a radius of

{{Math|formula = 28 \times \sqrt{ \frac{100 + 15\%}{100} } = 28 \times 1.07238 \approx 28 \times 1.07 = 29.96 }}

Similarly, {{Math|formula = \sqrt{ 1.16 } \approx 1.077}} is also truncated to 1.07, thus the breakpoint to increase a radius of 28 to 30 is '''17%''' increased AoE.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M6I_AB32Aa4</ref>

==Mechanics with both AoE and non-AoE aspects==
Some mechanics' effects may have both AoE and non-AoE aspects. Modifiers that apply to "area of effect" will only apply to the AoE aspect of these mechanics.

* One example is a {{il|Fire Trap}} skill gem supported by an {{il|Increased Area of Effect Support}} gem. The trap has a trigger radius, meaning it will explode if an enemy moves into a circular area of ground around the trap. This trigger area does not create any effects on the enemy or anything else within the area, so it is not considered to be an "area of effect." The support gem will not increase the size of the trigger area. However, when the trap triggers, it produces a circular area of [[burning ground]] that damages enemies on it. That ''is'' an "area of effect," so the support gem will increase its size.

* Another example is [[Earthbreaker Support]] totems. The totem is only active when the character is standing within a given radius of it. Standing in this circle of ground activates the totem, but doesn't create any effects on anything else in the circle, so the circle is not an "area of effect." Adding an {{il|Increased Area of Effect Support}} gem will not allow the character to activate the totem from farther away. It will increase the area of AoE skills used by the totem, however.

